Assignment 3 design commentary, written as code comments so it remains attached to
the prototype itself:

The chosen context for this browser prototype is a small harbor observatory exhibit
called Harbor Sky Calibration. The exhibit imagines that visitors are helping align
an instrument that translates waterfront beacon lights into a night-sky
constellation. I chose this context because it naturally supports a drag
interaction: calibration is a physical, spatial task, and dragging a point across a
field feels closer to tuning a real instrument than pressing a normal button would.
The brief asks for one focused browser interaction rather than a complete website,
so the whole screen is dedicated to the interactive surface. The header, progress
meter, and two icon controls are intentionally secondary; they frame the experience
without turning it into a conventional content page.

The visual style follows the observatory context. The palette uses deep night
values as a base, but it avoids relying on only one blue tone. Cyan, amber, rose,
and green appear as beacon colors because harbor equipment, instrument panels, and
signal lamps often communicate state through contrasting colored light. The bottom
of the page includes a quiet waterfront silhouette, which anchors the artwork in
the chosen setting while keeping the actual task clear. I avoided external images,
fonts, and code libraries so the project meets the assignment rules and can be
published as a simple GitHub Pages folder. The stars, targets, buildings, and
beacons are ordinary HTML elements styled with CSS.

Usability depends on feedback being layered rather than explained by a long block
of text. The draggable beacons are brighter and larger than the background stars.
When a mouse hovers over a beacon, the beacon gains emphasis and the cursor changes,
which suggests that it can be moved. While dragging, the status text changes as the
user gets closer to the target. When the user releases close enough to a target, the
beacon snaps into place, locks, and updates the progress meter. The snap threshold
is deliberately forgiving because an exhibit or art prototype should reward
exploration rather than require pixel-perfect precision. The hint button reveals
the complete target constellation for visitors who want a more explicit puzzle
state, while the default view keeps the targets subtle enough to feel like
discovery.

The implementation uses techniques covered in the course. The interactive objects
are buttons and div elements placed inside a stage with CSS positioning. JavaScript
selects the elements with querySelector and querySelectorAll, then uses
addEventListener to respond to mousedown, mousemove, mouseup, and click events. The
dragging calculation uses getBoundingClientRect together with clientX and clientY,
which matches the mouse-based dragging approach discussed in class. The element
positions are updated by changing style.left and style.top. Visual feedback is
handled with CSS transitions, classes, and simple DOM updates rather than external
libraries or advanced drawing tools.

If this prototype became part of a larger project, the main advantage would be the
clarity of its interaction model. Dragging spatial objects into alignment could
extend into an educational constellation builder, a museum kiosk, or a narrative
game mechanic. The strongest challenge would be adding more levels without
hard-coding every target. A simple data format for constellation positions, labels,
and feedback copy would let designers expand the experience more easily. Even with
those challenges, the core benefit is strong: a single browser gesture creates a
clear loop of intent, visual response, correction, and reward.

AI use statement:

I used AI in a limited way during this project as a study and writing support
tool. I mainly used it to help clarify small technical questions about
mouse-based dragging, such as how mouse coordinates can be compared with an
element's position on the page. I also used it to help phrase parts of my design
commentary more clearly. The concept, visual direction, interaction idea, layout
decisions, and final code decisions were made and edited by me.